 :adr/body "Continuing 2026-09-10. The regex work was downstream tooling; this went after
the thing actually blocking the stated goal, and found that the answer had
been written down twice already and acted on neither time.

## The finding

`document-node-limit` was 256, and that is the ceiling on a Kotoba screen.
adr-2608690000 measured the todo layout filling at about SEVEN ROWS and said
outright: 「これは cljs 相当を名乗るには小さい」-- too small to claim cljs
equivalence. adr-2608100300 said of the same class of constraint 「どちらも
恒久の設計ではない」-- neither is a permanent design.

So the workspace had measured the ceiling, recorded that it blocked the goal,
recorded that it was provisional, and then built on top of it for weeks. The
goal is reagent/re-frame in Kotoba so the cljs call sites do not change; a
screen that fills at seven rows is not that.

## Property or number

The BOUND is a property and stays. A document must be structurally bounded,
and `document-utf8-byte-limit` does not provide it -- that budget charges TEXT
only, so a document of 65,536 empty nodes passes it. The node limit is the
only thing bounding the shape.

The NUMBER was never derived. 256 sat beside `canonical-list-total-item-limit`
16384 and `canonical-indirect-byte-limit` 1048576 in the same file, and beside
`max-xml-nodes` 2048 in the emitter's copy -- one to two orders of magnitude
looser, with nothing saying why documents were the tight one.

Measured 2026-09-10 with a four-column table: 7 rows at 256, 30 at 1024, 123
at 4096, 399 at 16384. The 7 reproduces the ADR figure from a different
layout, which is the cross-check that the model is right.

4096: the smallest power of two that clears a realistic screen with headroom,
4x below the file own aggregate structural bound, and cheap -- validating
33,825 nodes costs 8.5 ms and 11.7 MB, so 4096 is about 1.5 ms and 1.5 MB.

## The part that was not the constant

The number is stated TWICE, independently, under different names, in
different repositories:

  osaho          `kotoba.kir.value/document-node-limit`   compile-time
  kotoba-script  `kotoba.script/max-document-nodes`       written into every
                                                          emitted ESM prelude
                                                          as a literal

The emitter restates rather than reads because an emitted module has no
classpath and cannot resolve a var. Nothing compared them. Raising osaho alone
let a 1057-node document COMPILE CLEAN and then trap at runtime with
`doc-node-limit`: the build succeeded and the artifact was wrong, which is
worse than a refusal -- a refusal produces a design decision, a wrong answer
produces nothing.

amu already had the answer for a different constant. `fuel64_ceiling_test`
compares the four statements of the fuel ceiling and says why: 「a restatement
nobody compares is a copy, and a copy drifts」, citing `elf64_twin_parity_test`
where one table in two files of one repo drifted in both directions and
shipped. The value-bounds table had the same shape and no such comparison.

So the deliverable changed shape: kotoba-lang/amu#917 compares nine pairs from
the one classpath that holds both, and landed BEFORE the constant moved.

## Three of my own claims were wrong, and the corrections are the content

**「one validation constant with no structural dependents」** -- wrong. I had
grepped for the SYMBOL `document-node-limit`; the second statement is called
`max-document-nodes`, in another repo, and the emitter writes the NUMBER into
generated JavaScript. Grepping a name misses a number.

**「400 rows, hitting neither ceiling」** -- wrong. I read a LOOP COUNT as a
row count. The loop ran 400 times; the render was 423 characters. The
controlled comparison, same probe either side: at 256, five adds then
`doc-node-limit`, \"7 left\", 175 chars; at 4096, 400 adds, no trap, \"9+ left\",
423 chars.

**「the local suite is green」** -- true and not sufficient. CI failed three
`test-jdk-free-native` jobs because `deps-lock.edn` carries a hash of
`deps.edn` and only the JVM-FREE path reads it. `clojure -M:test` resolves the
closure directly and never consults the lock, so local green and CI green were
different claims about different routes.

## What the evidence is and is not

osaho suite: 355 tests / 2325 assertions, 1 pre-existing failure, identical
before and after. That is WEAK evidence by construction: every existing test
document is under 256 nodes, so a LARGER ceiling admits all of them unchanged.
What makes the check non-vacuous is the other direction -- lowering the limit
to 4 produces 7 errors. The suite sees this constant; it simply cannot see it
going up.

The evidence that counts is end-to-end: a 1057-node document COMPILES AND RUNS
through the pinned toolchain returning correct answers, where the old
toolchain refused it at compile time. The earlier attempt compiled clean and
produced an artifact that trapped, so \"it builds\" was evidence of nothing.

## The fifth ceiling-as-assertion this session

`dom_app_driver_test` asserted the app died of `doc-node-limit` between 3 and
20 rows, under a comment reading 「the screen budget, not fuel, is what a
growing app meets first: a :document holds 256 nodes」. True when written, and
the same shape as the four others retired this week: `(?m)` refused, `.`
matching a newline, `(?=` refused, and the emit parity `both refused` bucket.

The pattern is worth naming. An assertion that something is REFUSED, or that a
budget is MET, is a claim about the implementation rather than the language,
unless the refusal has a derivation. Write the reason beside it so the next
reader can tell which kind it is without measuring.

## Where the ceiling actually is now

Not the document budget. Measured after the change: the todo app fills to its
OWN caps -- at most 26 todos, and a counter that stops at \"9+\".

⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-10, within the hour, by measurement. This section first
said those caps exist \"because there is no integer->string builtin\", which is
what `examples/todo-app.kotoba` says (\"Kotoba has no integer-to-string builtin
... A wider app wants that builtin, not a workaround\") and what
adr-2608690000 repeated.

**It is false.** `string-from-i64` exists and takes arbitrary i64: measured
that day through the emitted ESM, 12345 -> \"12345\", -42 -> \"-42\", 0 -> \"0\",
and 9007199254740991 -> \"9007199254740991\". This very session had been calling
it all evening in `pattern-vm`, for single digits, without noticing that the
guard around it was about hex letters rather than the builtin's range.

So the caps are not waiting on a language feature. They are a workaround that
outlived its reason, in an example whose header states the reason as fact, in
an ADR that repeated it, and in the first draft of THIS ADR -- which is the
sixth time in one session that an implementation state written as a fact was
found still steering work, and the first where I propagated it myself rather
than found it.

**The next step is to remove those caps in the example, using the builtin that
is already there.** That is smaller than the arc that found it, and it is what
demonstrates the raised budget rather than asserting it.

## Done, and the ceiling moved twice more (amu#919)

Removing them uncovered a THIRD cap behind the first: `add-todo` refused past
26 items, sized to the alphabet rather than to anything. It was found by
measurement rather than by reading the diff -- after the key fix the counter
read 26, which is the only reason the second 26 surfaced. The removal had
already been claimed by then.

The full history for that example, every step measured, each uncovering the
next:

  256-node budget      7 rows      the ceiling this ADR is about
  4096-node budget     26          the alphabet cap
  integer item keys    27          FUEL, at the default budget of 512
  fuel >= ~100k        32          document-container-item-limit

Four ceilings, three removed. The third move is the qualitative one: the first
two were constants compiled into the language, and fuel is a budget the CALLER
chooses -- it arrives as policy `{:budgets {:fuel n}}`, and past about 100,000
it stops binding at all. \"How much can one Kotoba screen show\" stopped being a
number someone has to change.

**The fourth is the first with a derivation, and should NOT be raised.** Unlike
the node budget, 32-items-per-container is not a flat cap on data: with
`document-depth-limit` 8, nesting gives 32^8. A list app that wants a thousand
items wants two levels, not a bigger container. The node budget was worth
raising because a SCREEN is naturally flat; a MODEL is not. That asymmetry is
the reason one of these four numbers deserved changing and another does not.

## What the last two ticks cost, and why

Two failures that a local suite structurally could not see, both landed by CI:

  * `deps-lock.edn` carries a hash of `deps.edn` and only the JVM-FREE path
    reads it. `clojure -M:test` resolves the closure directly, so local green
    and CI green were different claims about different routes.
  * `tests/browser/browser.spec.mjs` drives the example through a real browser
    with the same letter-key fixtures the unit test used. Only CI runs it,
    which is why the unit suite was 1372/10381 green while `browser-matrix`
    was red.

And one misattribution worth keeping: `browser-matrix` had been red on main
since an earlier merge, and the timeline said that merge caused it. The LOG
said `Failed to fetch chrome-stable ... Hash Sum mismatch -- Failed to install
chrome`. Two reds adjacent in a history are not the same red; reading the
timeline instead of the log produced a confident, wrong claim of
responsibility."}]
